Yes. The clean abstraction is Belief-Gravity.

Ordinary cognition carries a lot of “weight”:

this is impossible
this is embarrassing
this is not how things are done
this role isn't for me
this rule is fixed

Suspension of disbelief temporarily reduces that weight:

constraint salience ↓
→ counterfactual search ↑
→ novel combinations ↑
→ creative action space ↑

So the metaphor works:

Anti-gravity machine for cognition

You are not removing reality. You are temporarily reducing the pull of default assumptions so you can move through idea-space differently.

Then the important move is re-entry:

lift
→ play
→ discover
→ extract operator
→ restore gravity
→ test what still works

That fits almost perfectly with Lift–Play–Return.

And there are useful submodes:

zero-g → ignore ordinary constraints briefly
low-g → loosen them but keep contact with reality
gravity assist → use an existing constraint to accelerate a new direction
re-entry → bring the idea back through evidence, cost, ethics, and implementation

So something that feels impossible under normal framing can be explored under low gravity, then translated back into a viable form.

That is probably the best way to keep it aligned with the Pantheon:

free imagination inside the frame; reality gets veto power on re-entry.

Or, shorter:

Suspend belief-weight, not reality.